    <!--
        AMENDED 2026-08-09 BY DECISION D21 - Counter_Offer__c: viewAllRecords FALSE -> TRUE.
        allowEdit STAYS FALSE. THIS RESOLVES THE RESIDUAL THE BLOCK ABOVE REPORTED RATHER THAN
        ABSORBED, and that block is KEPT AS HISTORY. The full reasoning lives in the matching
        block on DPEG_Disposition_Edit; only what is DIFFERENT for this persona is written here.

        WHAT THIS GRANTS, STATED PLAINLY AND WITHOUT SOFTENING: a disposition persona can now READ
        EVERY COUNTER OFFER IN THE ORG, acquisition rows included - DPEG's own negotiating position
        on live PURCHASES, exposed to personas the 2026-07-22 RBAC build partitioned into the
        disposition module. Same class of informed widening as D12/W3 on NDA__c.

        🔴 THIS SET IS THE REASON THE DECISION WAS NEEDED AT ALL, WHICH IS WHY THE RATIONALE READS
        SLIGHTLY DIFFERENTLY HERE. The block above already identified this persona as the sharpest
        case: a PRINCIPAL CREATES NOTHING, so under Private OWD they do not even see rows by
        ownership the way the Edit persona does. With viewAllRecords false and no sharing rule
        available they saw ZERO counter offers - and the failure is SILENT, because an unshared row
        is FILTERED OUT of a WITH USER_MODE query rather than refused. CounterOfferSelector
        .selectByLoiId returned an empty list, so the card rendered an EMPTY negotiation history on
        a disposition LOI and an approver had no way to tell that apart from a deal that has had no
        counter rounds. An approver reading a silently empty history is the failure mode
        NDA_Disposition_Principals_R and LOI_Disposition_Principals_R were each written to prevent
        on their own objects; a visible-but-wide history beats an invisible one.

        WHY THE NARROW D17/D20.1 SHAPE WAS NOT USED - three blockers, each sufficient alone, all
        re-verified against this branch on 2026-08-09:
          1. Counter_Offer__c HAS NO RECORD TYPES (the object folder holds the object file and
             fields/ only), so the 'RecordTypeId equals ...Disposition_*' criterion both the NDA__c
             and LOI__c rules use has nothing to name.
          2. Direction__c CANNOT DISCRIMINATE: per its own token contract 'Seller' means THE
             COUNTERPARTY ON BOTH SIDES, so a rule keyed on 'Seller' or 'Ours' captures ACQUISITION
             rows, while one keyed on the disposition-only 'Buyer' MISSES every disposition row
             whose latest counter came from us.
          3. FORMULA FIELDS ARE NOT SUPPORTED IN CRITERIA-BASED SHARING RULE CRITERIA - a platform
             restriction, so deriving the side from the parent LOI in a formula is unavailable.

        🔴 THE NARROW LONG-TERM FIX, NAMED SO A FUTURE READER CAN FIND IT: a STORED DISCRIMINATOR
        CHECKBOX on Counter_Offer__c (an Is_Disposition__c) set by CounterOfferService on save,
        plus a criteria-based sharing rule keyed on that checkbox and a plain read in place of this
        grant. The service ALREADY COMPUTES THE VALUE - isSaleSide(parent) runs on every
        saveCounterOffer to pick the Buyer/Seller counterparty label - so the stamp costs no extra
        SOQL and no extra DML. It is the platform's own documented workaround for blocker 3.
        ⚠ It needs a BACKFILL as well as a field: pre-existing rows would read false and so be
        INVISIBLE under the new rule. Field plus automation plus backfill, then the swap.

        🔴 allowEdit IS DELIBERATELY FALSE AND MUST NOT BE "TIDIED" TO MATCH THE READ GRANT. This
        is the READ-ONLY Principal persona and its CRUD is untouched: allowCreate, allowEdit and
        allowDelete all stay false. Read and edit are separable on this object because
        CounterOfferService writes in SYSTEM MODE (plain DML, which does not consult the running
        persona's object CRUD), so nothing about the counter card depends on an edit grant here.
        Raising allowEdit to follow the widened read would let a disposition persona REWRITE the
        recorded history of a live acquisition negotiation - the exact compounding error D17 spent
        a decision unwinding on NDA__c earlier today, where viewAllRecords and allowEdit combined
        to reach past an acceptance argued on VISIBILITY GROUNDS ALONE. The same reduction was
        applied to DPEG_Disposition_Edit in this change, where allowEdit went TRUE to FALSE; the
        two files are aligned on Counter_Offer__c CRUD and both must move together.

        ================================================================================
        RECONCILIATION AGAINST THE ORG - PERFORMED THIS TIME, CORRECTING THE STANDING CAVEAT
        REPEATED IN THE TWO BLOCKS ABOVE.
        ================================================================================

        🔴 A PermissionSet DEPLOY REPLACES THIS SET'S ENTIRE GRANT LIST - it does not merge, and an
        org-side-only grant absent from this file is WIPED by the next deploy of it, even one made
        for an unrelated reason. The blocks above record that reconciliation was NOT performed and
        that "a retrieve did not return it, most likely because 3A is not deployed (D14) - an
        INFERENCE, not a measurement."

        THAT INFERENCE IS NOW FALSIFIED ON ITS FACTUAL HALF. A PermissionSet retrieve into a
        scratch directory on 2026-08-09 SUCCEEDED and returned this set: it EXISTS in usman-dpeg,
        created and last modified 2026-07-22T08:41:49Z, the RBAC build date. What remains true is
        that 3A/3B are undeployed, so the org copy is the 2026-07-22 BASELINE rather than absent.

        WHAT THE NORMALIZED DIFF MEASURED (org copy against this file, XML-parsed, order
        independent):
            org copy  : 5 objectPermissions, 62 fieldPermissions
            this file : 8 objectPermissions, 100 fieldPermissions
            ORG-ONLY objectPermissions ......... NONE
            ORG-ONLY fieldPermissions .......... NONE
            value differences on grants present in BOTH ... NONE
        So on the evidence of that retrieve there is nothing in this set org-side for a deploy of
        this file to wipe; every difference is an addition this branch makes.

        ⚠ SCOPE OF THAT CLAIM, STATED SO IT IS NOT OVER-READ: it concerns THIS PERMISSION SET as
        retrieved on 2026-08-09 and nothing else. It says nothing about profiles (profiles/** is
        .forceignore'd here, so profile-level FLS is invisible to any file-based check), nothing
        about other permission sets, and nothing about org state after that date. Re-run the
        retrieve and the diff immediately before deploying.
    -->
